<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Basohli State</h1><p class="st-meta">765 CE – 1836 CE (approx.) · capital Basohli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Rajput hill state on the Ravi whose court gave its name to the bold, brightly coloured Basohli school of Pahari painting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Basohli was ruled by a Balauria branch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>; tradition places the state's foundation in 765, but little is known before the 17th century. Raja Bhupat Pal is credited with founding the town of Basohli on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> as his capital in the 1630s.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under Raja Kirpal Pal, in the late 17th century, the court patronised painters who developed the Basohli style: strong reds, yellows and greens, flat backgrounds and jewel-like beetle-wing details, used for series such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasamanjari">Rasamanjari</a>. The style spread to neighbouring courts. Politically, Basohli was a minor state between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chamba-state">Chamba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the early 19th century Basohli fell under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> and was granted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a>, who absorbed it in 1836. It later became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jammu-and-kashmir-princely-state">Jammu and Kashmir (princely state)</a>. The town is now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathua">Kathua</a> district.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 765 CE – 1836 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:74.93%;width:21.31%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>765 CE – 1836 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Basohli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bhupat Pal, Kirpal Pal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dogri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Basohli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1300&amp;focus=basohli-state" aria-label="Basohli State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6817 1346 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Basohli State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6918 1285h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7137 1602h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7137 1602h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=1300&amp;focus=basohli-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

